  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStratocu under cold NW flow should gradually diminish through\nthis evening under building high pressure. The high should \nmaintain dry weather through Wednesday as it shifts eastward,\nthough mid and high clouds are expected to increase later in the\nday ahead of approaching low pressure. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA deepening upper low is expected to advance eastward from the\nMidwest on Thursday, as a developing surface low tracks north\nacross the Mid Atlantic region. Rain is expected to overspread\nthe region from S-N early Thursday, with increasing ascent and\nmoist advection. A jet just above the surface should also result\nin additional ascent, and also the potential for a downslope\nwind. It seems most likely that a Wind Advisory may be needed\nfor the higher terrain areas of PA and WV, and areas on the\nleeward side of the ridges. Gusts near warning criteria are also\npossible, though the latest probabilities indicate advisory\nlevel gusts from 45 to 50 mph are more likely. Held off on any\nwind products at this time, in coordination with surrounding NWS\noffices, with some uncertainty in the details. Model soundings\nindicate the strongest jet wind should remain above a\ntemperature inversion, though this inversion should also result\nin a mountain wave type downslope wind.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe lows are expected to become nearly vertically stacked\nThursday night into Friday as they track across western PA, into\nNew England. Rain should mix with snow across the lower\nelevations at times as colder air wraps around the low. The\nhigher terrain areas should change to predominantly snow through\nthe day Friday. The downslope wind potential should gradually \nend Thursday night, with a shift to west/southwesterly flow. The\nstrong wind potential will likely continue across portions of \nthe WV ridges into Friday, with a tight surface pressure \ngradient, mixing and cold advection. Rainfall amounts are \nexpected to average from 1-1.5 inches. Probabilities of at least\nan inch are currently around 70 percent, with 10-20 percent \nprobabilities of 2 inches. Snow accumulation across the higher\nterrain, especially across WV, could approach advisory levels\nshould temperatures cool faster Friday into Friday night.\nEnsemble probabilities indicate a 40-50 percent chance of seeing\n3 inches across Tucker county WV.",
